According to the paper, the fact that only Claude 3.5 Sonnet and GPT‑4o never hit the 9‑retry intervention threshold in Self Recovery implies that, relative to the other evaluated models, they

- were able to self‑correct errors without external hints — i.e., complete runs by internally tracing and fixing upstream mistakes;
- exhibited stronger sustained sequential reasoning and longer‑horizon memory use (better ability to reason about causal chains and revise earlier choices);
- were less reliant on the soft intervention (revealing the answer after repeated identical failures), so their task completions in Self Recovery are interpreted as coming from internal reasoning rather than prompted help.

Caveat (as the paper notes): this is the authors’ interpretation of the observed correlation, not a formal causal proof — other factors (e.g., different retry strategies, response stochasticity, or operational handling) could also influence reaching the threshold.
